{"title": "Contaminant transport in groundwater", "kind": "Process", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Hydrogeology#Contaminant transport properties", "definition": {"text": "The movement of dissolved contaminants with moving groundwater, studied by contaminant hydrogeology.", "sent_ids": ["35aa31fe050b"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Contaminants may be man-made, such as petroleum products, nitrate or radionuclides, or natural, such as arsenic or salinity.", "sent_ids": ["7f38334a2a55"]}, {"text": "They move by three main mechanisms: advection at seepage velocity, diffusion from high to low concentration, and hydrodynamic dispersion.", "sent_ids": ["7f38334a2a55"]}, {"text": "Aquifer properties beyond the flow itself affect how dissolved contaminants move.", "sent_ids": ["d3fa780db776"]}, {"text": "Diffusion and dispersion only spread a contaminant, while retardation by adsorption slows its average velocity.", "sent_ids": ["49184de33e02"]}, {"text": "Polluted groundwater forms a plume whose advancing edge can reach wells and springs; its movement is analysed with hydrological transport or groundwater models.", "sent_ids": ["85e7c3f7aaf6", "334c0a7779fc", "adebc27af23d"]}, {"text": "Diffusion, adsorption, precipitation and decay all influence pollutant transport.", "sent_ids": ["4a4e58810967"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-phys-203", "soil-phys-201", "soil-phys-208"], "related": ["soil-phys-193", "soil-phys-194", "soil-phys-196", "soil-phys-195", "soil-water-230"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Hydrodynamic dispersion", "Molecular diffusion of solutes", "Retardation by adsorption"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil physics", "chapter": "Solute transport in soil", "id": "soil-phys-192", "score": 20.24, "score_parts": {"importance": 6.664, "weight": 3, "tests": 3, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 6.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section", "mentioned on 2+ pages", "prerequisite of 2+"], "sources": [{"title": "Hydrogeology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogeology", "revid": 1374093353, "sent_ids": ["7f38334a2a55", "d3fa780db776", "49184de33e02", "35aa31fe050b"]}, {"title": "Groundwater", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groundwater", "revid": 1384924420, "sent_ids": ["85e7c3f7aaf6", "334c0a7779fc", "adebc27af23d", "4a4e58810967"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-phys-203", "soil-phys-201"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-phys-203": "ordering pass", "soil-phys-201": "ordering pass", "soil-phys-208": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 114, "position": 5}
{"title": "Advection", "kind": "Process", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Advection", "definition": {"text": "The transport of a substance or quantity by the bulk motion of a fluid.", "sent_ids": ["695cd42e5417"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It needs currents in a fluid, so it cannot happen in rigid solids, and it excludes transport by molecular diffusion.", "sent_ids": ["ee9dd68076ca", "22da174c4102"]}, {"text": "Ink dumped in a river moves downstream as a pulse by advection while also spreading by diffusion; the sum of the two is convection.", "sent_ids": ["4ead49ceb5cb", "aafd9cd20142", "c965a507fb4c"]}, {"text": "In groundwater, advection carries contaminants along the main direction of flow at the seepage velocity.", "sent_ids": ["7f38334a2a55"]}, {"text": "The advection equation is a first-order hyperbolic partial differential equation derived from the conservation law of the transported quantity.", "sent_ids": ["9478b346ae36", "f6651c50299d"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-phys-203"], "related": ["soil-phys-197", "soil-phys-192", "soil-phys-194"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil physics", "chapter": "Solute transport in soil", "id": "soil-phys-193", "score": 15.71, "score_parts": {"importance": 5.13, "weight": 3, "tests": 3, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 3.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages", "prerequisite of 2+"], "sources": [{"title": "Advection", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advection", "revid": 1370366240, "sent_ids": ["ee9dd68076ca", "22da174c4102", "4ead49ceb5cb", "aafd9cd20142", "c965a507fb4c", "9478b346ae36", "f6651c50299d", "695cd42e5417"]}, {"title": "Hydrogeology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogeology", "revid": 1374093353, "sent_ids": ["7f38334a2a55"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q379788", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-phys-203"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-phys-203": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 114, "position": 4}
{"title": "Hydrodynamic dispersion", "kind": "Process", "weight": "Major", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Hydrogeology#Hydrodynamic dispersion", "definition": {"text": "The spreading of dissolved contaminants away from the mean path of the groundwater carrying them, caused by small-scale heterogeneity and uneven velocities in the porous medium.", "sent_ids": ["7f38334a2a55", "c551feefb750"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Its strength is expressed by empirical dispersivities: longitudinal (αL) for solute ahead of or behind the mean flow, transverse (αT) for solute to the sides.", "sent_ids": ["c551feefb750", "e9bca62bc39b"]}, {"text": "At the microscopic scale, water passing each soil particle must go left, right, up or down, so solute spreads around the mean path.", "sent_ids": ["5079000e94cc", "d918a4f7a672"]}, {"text": "Over long distances, zones of higher and lower permeability give preferential paths that spread contaminants irregularly.", "sent_ids": ["943613f1a7c9"]}, {"text": "Dispersivity reflects details averaged away in a macroscopic model, such as thin gravel or clay beds in sand, so it is partly a measure of missing information.", "sent_ids": ["0e027d6be8e1", "276fe9c45529"]}, {"text": "It is therefore often claimed to depend on scale: dispersivity through 1 m3 of aquifer differs from that through 1 cm3 of the same material.", "sent_ids": ["77ba69a0f4f8"]}, {"text": "Salinity models need dispersivity as a soil input alongside water retention and unsaturated hydraulic conductivity.", "sent_ids": ["8688aed800f7", "6e662ae9f831"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-phys-193", "soil-phys-192", "soil-phys-063", "soil-water-157"], "related": ["soil-phys-195", "soil-phys-197", "soil-chem-016"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Contaminant transport in groundwater", "Molecular diffusion of solutes", "Retardation by adsorption"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil physics", "chapter": "Solute transport in soil", "id": "soil-phys-194", "score": 13.66, "score_parts": {"importance": 6.664, "weight": 3, "tests": 2, "pages": 2.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Hydrogeology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogeology", "revid": 1374093353, "sent_ids": ["c551feefb750", "e9bca62bc39b", "5079000e94cc", "d918a4f7a672", "943613f1a7c9", "0e027d6be8e1", "276fe9c45529", "77ba69a0f4f8", "7f38334a2a55"]}, {"title": "SaltMod", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SaltMod", "revid": 1361802436, "sent_ids": ["8688aed800f7"]}, {"title": "Soil salinity control", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_salinity_control", "revid": 1326443394, "sent_ids": ["6e662ae9f831"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-phys-193", "soil-phys-192", "soil-water-157"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-phys-193": "ordering pass", "soil-phys-192": "ordering pass", "soil-phys-063": "ordering pass", "soil-water-157": "consolidation"}, "unit": 115, "position": 1}
{"title": "Molecular diffusion of solutes", "kind": "Process", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Hydrogeology#Molecular diffusion", "definition": {"text": "The random thermal movement of molecules that carries solutes from high to low concentration.", "sent_ids": ["b74649c4b232", "7f38334a2a55"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Time to diffuse grows with the square of distance, so diffusion matters over small distances but spreads solutes slowly over large ones.", "sent_ids": ["16d386270e0a"]}, {"text": "Its coefficient is small and often negligible, except where groundwater flows extremely slowly, as in clay aquitards.", "sent_ids": ["fb664e9c1297"]}, {"text": "Diffusion is a physical process, whereas dispersion is an empirical factor written in the same mathematical form.", "sent_ids": ["e5aa7d4611be"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-phys-192", "soil-found-079"], "related": ["soil-phys-194", "soil-phys-148"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Contaminant transport in groundwater", "Hydrodynamic dispersion", "Retardation by adsorption"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil physics", "chapter": "Solute transport in soil", "id": "soil-phys-195", "score": 10.16, "score_parts": {"importance": 6.664, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 1.5}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Hydrogeology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogeology", "revid": 1374093353, "sent_ids": ["16d386270e0a", "fb664e9c1297", "e5aa7d4611be", "b74649c4b232", "7f38334a2a55"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-phys-192"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-phys-192": "ordering pass", "soil-found-079": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 115, "position": 3}
{"title": "Retardation by adsorption", "kind": "Process", "weight": "Major", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Hydrogeology#Retardation by adsorption", "definition": {"text": "The slowing of a contaminant's average movement relative to groundwater because it adsorbs to the soil, measured by a retardation factor.", "sent_ids": ["2c40a875ffcf", "557cb2b435cd"]}, "facts": [{"text": "The factor is analogous to the retardation factor of chromatography.", "sent_ids": ["bd99e274a63e"]}, {"text": "Adsorption holds the contaminant back until the amount set by the adsorption equilibrium has been adsorbed.", "sent_ids": ["557cb2b435cd"]}, {"text": "Less soluble contaminants can move hundreds or thousands of times slower than water, so only the more soluble species travel far.", "sent_ids": ["9783b493383d", "908d02ca7edb"]}, {"text": "The factor depends on the chemistry of both the contaminant and the aquifer.", "sent_ids": ["d76bde286996"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-phys-192", "soil-phys-193"], "related": ["soil-phys-193", "soil-chem-084", "soil-chem-068"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Contaminant transport in groundwater", "Hydrodynamic dispersion", "Molecular diffusion of solutes"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil physics", "chapter": "Solute transport in soil", "id": "soil-phys-196", "score": 11.66, "score_parts": {"importance": 6.664, "weight": 3, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Hydrogeology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogeology", "revid": 1374093353, "sent_ids": ["bd99e274a63e", "557cb2b435cd", "9783b493383d", "908d02ca7edb", "d76bde286996", "2c40a875ffcf"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-phys-192", "soil-chem-068"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-phys-192": "ordering pass", "soil-phys-193": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 115, "position": 2}
{"title": "Convection–diffusion equation", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Major", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Convection–diffusion equation", "definition": {"text": "A parabolic partial differential equation for a quantity moved by both diffusion and convection (advection).", "sent_ids": ["0c7492b8ab4a", "2feda6db89d9"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It is also called the advection–diffusion or scalar transport equation.", "sent_ids": ["92a561f9875e"]}, {"text": "It follows from the continuity equation, with diffusive flux from Fick's first law, −D∇c, and advective flux vc.", "sent_ids": ["3d1b2f05a330", "b9cf40d3f5a8", "586eec640efd"]}, {"text": "A source or sink term R adds or removes the quantity, as when a chemical reaction creates or destroys a species.", "sent_ids": ["3d8a728eb2d8", "be7c34353825"]}, {"text": "For flow in porous media, the velocity in the equation is the superficial velocity.", "sent_ids": ["d54f228ecd86"]}, {"text": "Most soil water and solute models, such as SWAP, DrainMod-S, UnSatChem and Hydrus, couple the Richards equation with it to move salts by advection and dispersion.", "sent_ids": ["3b4081f20dd6"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-phys-193", "soil-phys-195"], "related": ["soil-phys-194", "soil-water-238", "soil-phys-199", "soil-phys-181"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil physics", "chapter": "Solute transport in soil", "id": "soil-phys-197", "score": 11.3, "score_parts": {"importance": 3.219, "weight": 3, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 1.5}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Convection–diffusion equation", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convection%E2%80%93diffusion_equation", "revid": 1372339921, "sent_ids": ["92a561f9875e", "3d1b2f05a330", "b9cf40d3f5a8", "586eec640efd", "3d8a728eb2d8", "be7c34353825", "d54f228ecd86", "0c7492b8ab4a", "2feda6db89d9"]}, {"title": "Soil salinity control", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_salinity_control", "revid": 1326443394, "sent_ids": ["3b4081f20dd6"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q343279", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-phys-193", "soil-phys-195"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-phys-193": "ordering pass", "soil-phys-195": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 115, "position": 4}
{"title": "Colloid-facilitated transport", "kind": "Process", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Colloid-facilitated transport", "definition": {"text": "Transport in which colloidal particles carry contaminants through surface water and groundwater.", "sent_ids": ["d7d9750fb22d"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Colloids also move through surface soils and the ground depending on soil structure, compaction and particle size; the process drew real attention only in the 1980s.", "sent_ids": ["9a6ebc099d0c"]}, {"text": "Radionuclides, heavy metals and organic pollutants sorb easily onto suspended colloids, which may be inorganic (clays, iron oxy-hydroxides) or organic (humic and fulvic substances).", "sent_ids": ["6798fff03198", "b644e9b20387"]}, {"text": "Colloids are suspected in long-range plutonium transport at the Nevada Nuclear Test Site.", "sent_ids": ["e740c44e1528"]}, {"text": "Inorganic colloids barely move in compacted bentonite and deep clay, which ultrafilter them.", "sent_ids": ["c460557c7169"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-phys-192"], "related": ["soil-phys-196", "soil-bio-089", "soil-env-001", "soil-chem-068", "soil-chem-084", "soil-chem-154"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil physics", "chapter": "Solute transport in soil", "id": "soil-phys-198", "score": 2.0, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Colloid-facilitated transport", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colloid-facilitated_transport", "revid": 1218778165, "sent_ids": ["9a6ebc099d0c", "6798fff03198", "b644e9b20387", "e740c44e1528", "c460557c7169", "d7d9750fb22d"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q3997849", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-chem-084", "soil-phys-192"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-phys-192": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 116, "position": 3}
{"title": "Vadose zone transport models", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": null, "definition": {"text": "Computer models that simulate water flow and solute transport in unsaturated soil by solving the Richards equation numerically.", "sent_ids": ["b1da5cbe1bef"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Hydrus takes soil hydraulic parameters for the water retention and unsaturated conductivity functions, plus initial and boundary conditions.", "sent_ids": ["b1da5cbe1bef"]}, {"text": "Most such models, including SWAP, DrainMod-S, UnSatChem and Hydrus, join the Richards equation to a convection–diffusion equation for salts.", "sent_ids": ["3b4081f20dd6"]}, {"text": "The one-dimensional HYDRUS1D program is available online and used to estimate groundwater recharge.", "sent_ids": ["c210859700fc"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-water-238", "soil-phys-197", "soil-water-230"], "related": ["soil-chem-016", "soil-water-213", "soil-phys-225"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil physics", "chapter": "Solute transport in soil", "id": "soil-phys-199", "score": 3.0, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 2.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Groundwater recharge", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groundwater_recharge", "revid": 1329522257, "sent_ids": ["c210859700fc"]}, {"title": "Soil moisture", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_moisture", "revid": 1379027994, "sent_ids": ["b1da5cbe1bef"]}, {"title": "Soil salinity control", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_salinity_control", "revid": 1326443394, "sent_ids": ["3b4081f20dd6"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-water-238", "soil-phys-197"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-water-238": "ordering pass", "soil-phys-197": "ordering pass", "soil-water-230": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 116, "position": 2}
{"title": "Dye tracing", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Dye tracing", "definition": {"text": "A method of tracking flows by adding dye to a liquid as a tracer.", "sent_ids": ["078afe6b8676"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It can be qualitative, showing a flow exists, or quantitative, measuring dye amounts with instruments such as fluorometers.", "sent_ids": ["a44597ce679b", "644abbeeeed8"]}, {"text": "Fluorescein, first developed in 1871, and its sodium salt uranine remain among the best tracers, with rhodamine, pyranine and sulforhodamine B.", "sent_ids": ["1c4ac8189fbb", "7b46c77d4685", "d3167eb4e64b"]}, {"text": "Organic matter, other chemicals, sunlight and acidic water affect dye performance.", "sent_ids": ["f9c42b232c12", "25ad567699d4"]}, {"text": "Dye traces showed karst groundwater moving 0.8 to 11.3 km a day and that models assuming uniform porosity do not fit karst aquifers.", "sent_ids": ["2435fc75908e", "20b08fed26c0"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-phys-203", "soil-water-157"], "related": ["soil-water-157", "soil-phys-201", "soil-phys-192"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil physics", "chapter": "Solute transport in soil", "id": "soil-phys-200", "score": 4.97, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Dye tracing", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dye_tracing", "revid": 1179288045, "sent_ids": ["a44597ce679b", "644abbeeeed8", "1c4ac8189fbb", "7b46c77d4685", "d3167eb4e64b", "f9c42b232c12", "25ad567699d4", "078afe6b8676"]}, {"title": "Aquifer", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquifer", "revid": 1384979434, "sent_ids": ["2435fc75908e", "20b08fed26c0"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "In 1871", "date_ma": 7.9e-05, "sent_ids": ["1c4ac8189fbb"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q592181", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-phys-203"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-phys-203": "ordering pass", "soil-water-157": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 116, "position": 1}
